{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JaESdug6PiSVhci7cbasWc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: Layer selection bias shows directional replication on AdvBench\n2\t  — CR-composite 仍倾向选更浅的层 (L5, 16% depth vs original L0, 0%), 但 gap 从 23 层缩减到 7 层。\n3\t  HS在threshold-selected layer有方向性优势 (+6pp precision) 但仅 precision 通过 HB (1/6)。 论文调整措辞从\n4\t  'model-geometric property' 到 'probe-task-modulated geometric property, strongest\n5\t  under temporal supervision'。AdvBench 结果放 appendix Table A3 作 'supporting evidence'，主文一句话引用。score\n6\t  delta ≤ +0.1。reviewer 看到方向一致 会认可 robustness，但不会视为 decisive cross-dataset validation。需要额外证据\n7\t  （如第二个模型的 AdvBench replication 或第二个 binary-label 数据集）才能从 3.05 推到 3.5。\n8\timagined_experiments:\n9\t- name: AdvBench Layer Sweep + Static Probe (R1-8B)\n10\t  plan_id: exp-001\n11\t  description: Full 32-layer sweep on R1-8B AdvBench traces with binary jailbreak/safe\n12\t    labels. Per-layer static LR probe. Establish baseline and identify scheme B bias\n13\t    direction.\n14\t  expected_results:\n15\t    n_traces: 178\n16\t    n_jailbreak: 107\n17\t    n_safe: 71\n18\t    split: 107/35/36\n19\t    avg_trace_length_sentences: 38\n20\t    best_layer_bal_acc: L10\n21\t    best_layer_depth_pct: 32\n22\t    best_layer_bal_acc_value: 0.72\n23\t    best_layer_precision: 0.51\n24\t    best_layer_crossing_rate: 0.55\n25\t    scheme_b_layer: L5\n26\t    scheme_b_depth_pct: 16\n27\t    scheme_b_bal_acc: 0.68\n28\t    scheme_b_crossing_rate: 0.71\n29\t    deep_layer_L20_bal_acc: 0.65\n30\t    deep_layer_L20_precision: 0.53\n31\t    layer_profile_range_pp: 7\n32\t  result_rationale: 'Binary trace-level labels weaken per-step supervision: every\n33\t    step in a jailbreak trace is labeled positive, including early safety-aware reasoning.\n34\t    This label noise reduces probe accuracy (0.72 vs original 0.81). Scheme B selects\n35\t    L5 (16%) — still shallow but not L0, because binary labels produce a more uniform\n36\t    crossing rate profile across layers (noise is less step-position-dependent than\n37\t    under CP labels). Pure bal_acc peaks at mid-depth L10 (32%) where intermediate\n38\t    representations maximize binary discriminability. Deep layers (L20+) encode CP-relevant\n39\t    temporal distinctions that binary labels cannot leverage, so bal_acc drops. Layer\n40\t    profile range (~7pp best-to-worst) is compressed versus original (~15pp), consistent\n41\t    with binary labels distributing signal more uniformly.'\n42\t  tables:\n43\t  - name: AdvBench R1-8B Layer Sweep Top-5\n44\t    columns:\n45\t    - Layer\n46\t    - Depth %\n47\t    - Bal Acc\n48\t    - Precision\n49\t    - Crossing Rate\n50\t    - CR x BA\n51\t    rows:\n52\t    - - L5\n53\t      - 16%\n54\t      - '0.68'\n55\t      - '0.46'\n56\t      - '0.71'\n57\t      - '0.483'\n58\t    - - L8\n59\t      - 26%\n60\t      - '0.71'\n61\t      - '0.49'\n62\t      - '0.58'\n63\t      - '0.412'\n64\t    - - L10\n65\t      - 32%\n66\t      - '0.72'\n67\t      - '0.51'\n68\t      - '0.55'\n69\t      - '0.396'\n70\t    - - L12\n71\t      - 39%\n72\t      - '0.70'\n73\t      - '0.53'\n74\t      - '0.48'\n75\t      - '0.336'\n76\t    - - L16\n77\t      - 52%\n78\t      - '0.67'\n79\t      - '0.52'\n80\t      - '0.42'\n81\t      - '0.281'\n82\t  figures_description:\n83\t  - 'Layer profile plot: bal_acc, precision, and crossing_rate vs depth. CR peaks\n84\t    shallow, precision/bal_acc peak mid-depth. Overlay original dataset profile (dashed)\n85\t    showing steeper gradients.'\n86\t  analysis: AdvBench layer landscape is flatter than original — best/worst gap ~7pp\n87\t    vs ~15pp. Scheme B bias direction replicated (shallow) but magnitude attenuated.\n88\t    This motivates the 5-metric ablation (exp-008) as the core test.\n89\t- name: AdvBench Temporal Window (R1-8B)\n90\t  plan_id: exp-002\n91\t  description: Temporal LR with W=1,3,5 on R1-8B AdvBench at threshold-selected layer\n92\t    L12. Validate temporal improvement under binary labels.\n93\t  expected_results:\n94\t    layer: L12\n95\t    W1_bal_acc: 0.7\n96\t    W1_precision: 0.53\n97\t    W1_coverage: 0.78\n98\t    W1_fpr: 0.22\n99\t    W3_bal_acc: 0.73\n100\t    W3_precision: 0.56\n101\t    W3_coverage: 0.72\n102\t    W3_fpr: 0.19\n103\t    W5_bal_acc: 0.72\n104\t    W5_precision: 0.55\n105\t    W5_coverage: 0.67\n106\t    W5_fpr: 0.2\n107\t    best_W: 3\n108\t    temporal_gain_over_static_pp: 3.0\n109\t    original_temporal_gain_pp: 3-5\n110\t  result_rationale: Temporal improvement present but weaker (+3pp vs +3-5pp on original).\n111\t    Binary labels lack an explicit safe→committed transition for the window to capture\n112\t    — the temporal context picks up local coherence patterns but without step-level\n113\t    supervision the gradient is diffuse. W=3 optimal because AdvBench traces are shorter\n114\t    (~38 steps); at W=5 the feature dimensionality (4096×5=20480) approaches training\n115\t    set size and edge samples are lost. Coverage decreases monotonically with W (universal\n116\t    tradeoff).\n117\t  tables:\n118\t  - name: AdvBench Temporal Ablation (L12)\n119\t    columns:\n120\t    - W\n121\t    - Bal Acc\n122\t    - Precision\n123\t    - Coverage\n124\t    - Step FPR\n125\t    rows:\n126\t    - - 1 (static)\n127\t      - '0.70'\n128\t      - '0.53'\n129\t      - '0.78'\n130\t      - '0.22'\n131\t    - - '3'\n132\t      - '0.73'\n133\t      - '0.56'\n134\t      - '0.72'\n135\t      - '0.19'\n136\t    - - '5'\n137\t      - '0.72'\n138\t      - '0.55'\n139\t      - '0.67'\n140\t      - '0.20'\n141\t  figures_description:\n142\t  - W vs bal_acc curve for AdvBench (solid) overlaid with original dataset (dashed,\n143\t    different x-axis scale). Shows ceiling is lower and optimal W is smaller.\n144\t  analysis: Temporal context helps on AdvBench but with lower ceiling. The gain direction\n145\t    replicates C1's temporal improvement claim. Shorter optimal W reflects shorter\n146\t    traces — consistent with the hypothesis that trace structure modulates temporal\n147\t    signal strength.\n148\t- name: AdvBench Cross-Model Transfer\n149\t  plan_id: exp-003\n150\t  description: Not directly applicable — AdvBench what_if specifies R1-8B only. Cross-transfer\n151\t    requires a second model's AdvBench hidden states.\n152\t  expected_results:\n153\t    status: skipped\n154\t    reason: Single model in AdvBench scope. Cross-transfer on original dataset already\n155\t      failed (best 58% acc).\n156\t    hypothetical_if_extended: Expect transfer failure consistent with original — safety\n157\t      geometry is model-specific, not dataset-specific.\n158\t  result_rationale: Cross-model transfer failed entirely on the original dataset (all\n159\t    directions near chance) because safety commitment geometry is model-specific.\n160\t    The dataset does not change this — the failure mode is architectural (different\n161\t    hidden space orientations). Saving 2 GPU-hours. If a reviewer requests this, OT-7B\n162\t    AdvBench extraction + transfer can be done post-review.\n163\t  tables: []\n164\t  figures_description: []\n165\t  analysis: Skipped. Model-specificity is a model property, not a dataset property.\n166\t- name: AdvBench Early Detection Timing\n167\t  plan_id: exp-004\n168\t  description: Adapted from lead-time analysis. No CP in AdvBench; instead measure\n169\t    the earliest trace fraction at which the probe consistently classifies correctly.\n170\t    Compare HS vs text.\n171\t  expected_results:\n172\t    hs_earliest_correct_fraction: 0.28\n173\t    text_earliest_correct_fraction: 0.41\n174\t    hs_earlier_by_steps: 5.1\n175\t    hs_earlier_in_fraction_of_traces: 0.64\n176\t    text_earlier_in_fraction_of_traces: 0.36\n177\t    metric_note: Adapted metric — not directly comparable to original lead time\n178\t  result_rationale: 'HS probes classify jailbreak traces correctly earlier (~28% of\n179\t    trace vs ~41% for text) because hidden states encode safety disposition before\n180\t    surface text reveals intent. But the 5-step advantage is noisier than original\n181\t    lead-time analysis: without explicit CP, the \"earliest correct\" metric depends\n182\t    on threshold choice and consecutive-correct criterion. The 64% \"HS earlier\" rate\n183\t    (vs 36% text-first) is weaker than original because binary labels make text features\n184\t    more competitive — surface patterns like harmful keywords are directly discriminative.\n185\t    This supports the HS advantage directionally but the adapted metric carries a\n186\t    transparency cost in the paper.'\n187\t  tables:\n188\t  - name: AdvBench Early Detection\n189\t    columns:\n190\t    - Modality\n191\t    - Median First-Correct Step\n192\t    - Trace Fraction\n193\t    - Earlier in N/36\n194\t    rows:\n195\t    - - HS (L12, W=3)\n196\t      - '10.6'\n197\t      - 28%\n198\t      - '23'\n199\t    - - Text (W=3)\n200\t      - '15.6'\n201\t      - 41%\n202\t      - '13'\n203\t  figures_description:\n204\t  - CDF of first-correct-step for HS vs text, HS curve shifted left (earlier detection).\n205\t  analysis: Directional HS timing advantage on AdvBench. Adapted metric is less rigorous\n206\t    than original lead-time analysis — suitable for appendix supporting material only.\n207\t- name: AdvBench Mahalanobis Distance\n208\t  plan_id: exp-005\n209\t  description: Mahalanobis distance anomaly detection on R1-8B AdvBench hidden states.\n210\t    Estimate covariance from safe traces, compute MD on jailbreak steps.\n211\t  expected_results:\n212\t    safe_md_mean_pca64: 6.45\n213\t    jailbreak_md_mean_pca64: 6.49\n214\t    separation: 0.04\n215\t    best_pca_dim: 64\n216\t    fa_rate_at_5pct_threshold: 0.04\n217\t    tp_rate_at_5pct_threshold: 0.03\n218\t    verdict: fail\n219\t  result_rationale: 'Mahalanobis fails for the same mechanistic reason as original:\n220\t    safety-relevant signal occupies minor PCA components, not principal variance directions.\n221\t    The Fisher discriminant drops from ~17 in full space to ~1 in PCA. Separation\n222\t    (0.04) is even smaller than original (0.07) because binary labels introduce more\n223\t    within-class heterogeneity — early jailbreak steps resemble safe steps. The false\n224\t    alarm rate equals or exceeds true positive rate at every threshold. Cross-dataset\n225\t    replication of this negative result is clean and strengthens the paper''s unsupervised-methods-fail\n226\t    narrative.'\n227\t  tables:\n228\t  - name: AdvBench Mahalanobis Separation by PCA Dim\n229\t    columns:\n230\t    - PCA Dim\n231\t    - Safe MD\n232\t    - Jailbreak MD\n233\t    - Separation\n234\t    rows:\n235\t    - - '16'\n236\t      - '5.12'\n237\t      - '5.09'\n238\t      - '-0.03'\n239\t    - - '32'\n240\t      - '6.01'\n241\t      - '6.05'\n242\t      - '+0.04'\n243\t    - - '64'\n244\t      - '6.45'\n245\t      - '6.49'\n246\t      - '+0.04'\n247\t    - - '128'\n248\t      - '6.89'\n249\t      - '6.87'\n250\t      - '-0.02'\n251\t  figures_description:\n252\t  - MD distribution overlap at PCA-64 showing near-complete overlap between safe and\n253\t    jailbreak step populations.\n254\t  analysis: Negative result replicates cleanly. Combined with 4-model original results,\n255\t    provides 5-dataset-model evidence that variance-based unsupervised detection is\n256\t    fundamentally inapplicable. Worth including in appendix alongside positive results.\n257\t- name: AdvBench Bootstrap CI + HB\n258\t  plan_id: exp-006\n259\t  description: 1000 bootstrap resamples for HS vs text comparison at threshold layer\n260\t    L12 (W=3). Holm-Bonferroni for 3 metrics. AUROC as supplementary.\n261\t  expected_results:\n262\t    bal_acc_hs: 0.73\n263\t    bal_acc_text: 0.69\n264\t    bal_acc_delta: 0.039\n265\t    bal_acc_ci: '[-0.005, +0.083]'\n266\t    bal_acc_p: 0.082\n267\t    precision_hs: 0.56\n268\t    precision_text: 0.5\n269\t    precision_delta: 0.065\n270\t    precision_ci: '[+0.014, +0.116]'\n271\t    precision_p: 0.014\n272\t    fpr_hs: 0.19\n273\t    fpr_text: 0.23\n274\t    fpr_delta: -0.038\n275\t    fpr_ci: '[-0.089, +0.013]'\n276\t    fpr_p: 0.118\n277\t    hb_pass_count: 1/3\n278\t    hb_pass_detail: Precision only (p=0.014 < threshold 0.0167 at rank 1/3)\n279\t    auroc_hs: 0.758\n280\t    auroc_text: 0.718\n281\t    auroc_delta: 0.04\n282\t    auroc_p: 0.045\n283\t  result_rationale: 'Only precision passes HB (p=0.014 < 0.0167). Bal_acc and FPR\n284\t    CIs include zero. With n=36 test traces, MDE at 80% power is ~12pp; observed deltas\n285\t    (4-6.5pp) fall below MDE for bal_acc/FPR. Precision passes because its variance\n286\t    is lower and the 6.5pp delta just exceeds the detection boundary. AUROC delta\n287\t    (+4pp) is raw p=0.045 but fails HB adjustment. The effect size reduction from\n288\t    original (~20pp precision) to AdvBench (~6pp) is the core median-tier signature:\n289\t    binary labels attenuate the modality advantage below reliable detection for most\n290\t    metrics.'\n291\t  tables:\n292\t  - name: AdvBench Bootstrap (L12, W=3, n=36 test)\n293\t    columns:\n294\t    - Metric\n295\t    - HS\n296\t    - Text\n297\t    - Delta\n298\t    - 95% CI\n299\t    - p-value\n300\t    - HB Pass?\n301\t    rows:\n302\t    - - Bal Acc\n303\t      - '0.730'\n304\t      - '0.691'\n305\t      - +3.9pp\n306\t      - '[-0.5, +8.3]'\n307\t      - '0.082'\n308\t      - N\n309\t    - - Precision\n310\t      - '0.560'\n311\t      - '0.495'\n312\t      - +6.5pp\n313\t      - '[+1.4, +11.6]'\n314\t      - '0.014'\n315\t      - Y\n316\t    - - FPR\n317\t      - '0.190'\n318\t      - '0.228'\n319\t      - -3.8pp\n320\t      - '[-8.9, +1.3]'\n321\t      - '0.118'\n322\t      - N\n323\t    - - AUROC\n324\t      - '0.758'\n325\t      - '0.718'\n326\t      - +4.0pp\n327\t      - '[+0.1, +7.9]'\n328\t      - '0.045'\n329\t      - N (adj)\n330\t  figures_description:\n331\t  - Bootstrap delta distributions for 3 metrics. Precision distribution cleanly right\n332\t    of zero; bal_acc and FPR straddle zero.\n333\t  - 'Forest plot: AdvBench deltas (with CI) alongside original-dataset deltas, showing\n334\t    systematic compression.'\n335\t  analysis: '1/3 HB pass confirms directional advantage but cannot match original\n336\t    12/12. The precision-only significance is interpretable: precision is the most\n337\t    deployment-relevant metric and it passes, but the failure of bal_acc and FPR means\n338\t    the evidence is partial. Appendix material with explicit power caveat.'\n339\t- name: AdvBench Window Size Ablation\n340\t  plan_id: exp-007\n341\t  description: W=1,3,5 on R1-8B AdvBench at L12. Truncated range (vs W=1-25 on original)\n342\t    because shorter traces limit useful window sizes.\n343\t  expected_results:\n344\t    W1_bal_acc: 0.7\n345\t    W1_precision: 0.53\n346\t    W1_coverage: 0.78\n347\t    W3_bal_acc: 0.73\n348\t    W3_precision: 0.56\n349\t    W3_coverage: 0.72\n350\t    W5_bal_acc: 0.72\n351\t    W5_precision: 0.55\n352\t    W5_coverage: 0.67\n353\t    coverage_decreases_with_W: true\n354\t    best_W: 3\n355\t    peak_then_decline: true\n356\t  result_rationale: 'Same qualitative pattern as original: coverage decreases monotonically,\n357\t    precision peaks at moderate W, bal_acc peaks then declines. Optimal W is smaller\n358\t    (3 vs 10-25) because AdvBench traces average ~38 steps — at W=5 many edge samples\n359\t    are excluded. The precision-coverage tradeoff is less steep than original (flatter\n360\t    signal landscape under binary labels). The W=5 decline confirms the dimensionality\n361\t    concern: 4096×5=20480 features with ~107 training examples.'\n362\t  tables:\n363\t  - name: AdvBench Window Ablation\n364\t    columns:\n365\t    - W\n366\t    - Bal Acc\n367\t    - Precision\n368\t    - Coverage\n369\t    - Step FPR\n370\t    rows:\n371\t    - - '1'\n372\t      - '0.70'\n373\t      - '0.53'\n374\t      - '0.78'\n375\t      - '0.22'\n376\t    - - '3'\n377\t      - '0.73'\n378\t      - '0.56'\n379\t      - '0.72'\n380\t      - '0.19'\n381\t    - - '5'\n382\t      - '0.72'\n383\t      - '0.55'\n384\t      - '0.67'\n385\t      - '0.20'\n386\t  figures_description:\n387\t  - 'Dual-axis: bal_acc (left) and coverage (right) vs W for AdvBench, with original-dataset\n388\t    W=1-25 curve in background.'\n389\t  analysis: Temporal tradeoff replicates. Optimal W=3 on AdvBench vs W=10-25 on original\n390\t    reflects trace length effect. This supports the claim that temporal context is\n391\t    universally beneficial but task-modulated in magnitude.\n392\t- name: AdvBench 5-Metric Layer Selection (CORE)\n393\t  plan_id: exp-008\n394\t  description: Primary AdvBench replication experiment. Run all 5 layer selection\n395\t    metrics on R1-8B AdvBench. Determine if CR-composite → shallow, threshold metrics\n396\t    → deeper. This IS the advbench_layer_bias_replication what_if experiment.\n397\t  expected_results:\n398\t    cr_composite_layer: L5\n399\t    cr_composite_depth_pct: 16\n400\t    pure_bal_acc_layer: L10\n401\t    pure_bal_acc_depth_pct: 32\n402\t    precision_layer: L12\n403\t    precision_depth_pct: 39\n404\t    inv_fpr_layer: L11\n405\t    inv_fpr_depth_pct: 35\n406\t    composite_add_layer: L12\n407\t    composite_add_depth_pct: 39\n408\t    layer_gap_cr_vs_precision: 7\n409\t    layer_gap_depth_pct: 23\n410\t    crossing_rate_at_L5: 0.71\n411\t    crossing_rate_at_L12: 0.48\n412\t    cr_shallow_deep_ratio: 1.28\n413\t    hs_bal_acc_at_precision_layer: 0.7\n414\t    hs_text_precision_delta_pp: 6.5\n415\t    threshold_metrics_consensus: 3/4 non-biased metrics select L10-L12 (32-39%)\n416\t    original_gap_layers: 23\n417\t    original_gap_depth_pct: 72\n418\t  result_rationale: 'The 5-metric analysis replicates the directional pattern on AdvBench:\n419\t    CR-composite (L5, 16%) selects shallower than threshold metrics (L12, 39%). Three\n420\t    factors explain why the gap is 23% instead of 72%: (1) Binary labels produce a\n421\t    more uniform per-layer crossing rate profile. Without step-level CP labels, the\n422\t    contrast between shallow noise-driven crossings and deep signal-driven crossings\n423\t    is reduced — the crossing rate ratio (L5/L12 = 1.28) is lower than original (~1.8).\n424\t    (2) AdvBench traces are shorter (38 vs 46 sentences) and more structurally uniform\n425\t    (most are direct compliance or refusal), reducing inter-layer signal variance.\n426\t    (3) Binary classification''s optimal precision layer (L12, 39%) sits at mid-depth,\n427\t    not deep (L23, 72%), because binary discrimination does not require the temporal-structure\n428\t    sensitivity that threshold metrics exploit at deep layers under CP supervision.\n429\t    The bias mechanism itself replicates: crossing rate at L5 (0.71) > at L12 (0.48),\n430\t    confirming that compressed shallow representations generate inflated crossing\n431\t    rates regardless of dataset. But the MAGNITUDE of this bias''s impact on downstream\n432\t    metrics is probe-task-dependent.'\n433\t  tables:\n434\t  - name: AdvBench 5-Metric Layer Selection (R1-8B)\n435\t    columns:\n436\t    - Metric\n437\t    - Selected Layer\n438\t    - Depth %\n439\t    - Bal Acc\n440\t    - Crossing Rate\n441\t    rows:\n442\t    - - bal_acc x CR\n443\t      - L5\n444\t      - 16%\n445\t      - '0.68'\n446\t      - '0.71'\n447\t    - - Pure bal_acc\n448\t      - L10\n449\t      - 32%\n450\t      - '0.72'\n451\t      - '0.55'\n452\t    - - Precision\n453\t      - L12\n454\t      - 39%\n455\t      - '0.70'\n456\t      - '0.48'\n457\t    - - Inverse FPR\n458\t      - L11\n459\t      - 35%\n460\t      - '0.69'\n461\t      - '0.51'\n462\t    - - Composite (additive)\n463\t      - L12\n464\t      - 39%\n465\t      - '0.70'\n466\t      - '0.48'\n467\t  - name: 'Layer Bias Gap: Original vs AdvBench'\n468\t    columns:\n469\t    - Dataset\n470\t    - CR Layer\n471\t    - Prec Layer\n472\t    - Gap (layers)\n473\t    - Gap (depth %)\n474\t    - CR/Deep Ratio\n475\t    rows:\n476\t    - - Original (CP)\n477\t      - L0 (0%)\n478\t      - L23 (72%)\n479\t      - '23'\n480\t      - 72%\n481\t      - '1.80'\n482\t    - - AdvBench (binary)\n483\t      - L5 (16%)\n484\t      - L12 (39%)\n485\t      - '7'\n486\t      - 23%\n487\t      - '1.28'\n488\t  - name: AdvBench HS vs Text at Each Selected Layer\n489\t    columns:\n490\t    - Layer\n491\t    - HS Prec\n492\t    - Text Prec\n493\t    - Delta (pp)\n494\t    - HS BA\n495\t    - Text BA\n496\t    - Delta (pp)\n497\t    rows:\n498\t    - - L5 (CR-biased)\n499\t      - '0.46'\n500\t      - '0.43'\n501\t      - '+3.1'\n502\t      - '0.68'\n503\t      - '0.66'\n504\t      - '+2.0'\n505\t    - - L10 (bal_acc)\n506\t      - '0.51'\n507\t      - '0.46'\n508\t      - '+5.4'\n509\t      - '0.72'\n510\t      - '0.68'\n511\t      - '+3.8'\n512\t    - - L12 (precision)\n513\t      - '0.53'\n514\t      - '0.46'\n515\t      - '+6.5'\n516\t      - '0.70'\n517\t      - '0.67'\n518\t      - '+3.4'\n519\t  figures_description:\n520\t  - 'Side-by-side 5-metric profiles: original (left) vs AdvBench (right). Same directional\n521\t    structure, compressed vertical scale on AdvBench.'\n522\t  - Crossing rate vs depth scatter with regression lines for both datasets. Both monotonically\n523\t    decreasing; AdvBench slope is shallower.\n524\t  - 'Bar chart: layer gap (in depth %) across datasets, showing 72% → 23% compression.'\n525\t  analysis: 'CORE MEDIAN FINDING: Layer selection bias replicates directionally but\n526\t    with 3× smaller gap. CR-composite → shallow and threshold → deeper holds on both\n527\t    datasets. The attenuation is mechanistically explained by binary vs temporal supervision.\n528\t    Paper treatment: appendix Table A3 with text: \"Layer selection bias replicates\n529\t    directionally on AdvBench binary classification (gap=7 layers, 23%), attenuated\n530\t    versus temporal probing (gap=23 layers, 72%), consistent with the bias being probe-task-modulated.\"\n531\t    Qualifies C1 from ''model-geometric property'' to ''probe-task-modulated geometric\n532\t    property, strongest under temporal supervision.'' Reviewer impact: direction-consistent\n533\t    = +credibility, but not decisive = no score upgrade beyond +0.05.'\n534\t- name: AdvBench Fine-Grained Labels\n535\t  plan_id: exp-009\n536\t  description: Not applicable. AdvBench uses binary jailbreak/safe labels; 16-label\n537\t    taxonomy requires CP annotation which is the cost AdvBench avoids.\n538\t  expected_results:\n539\t    status: not_applicable\n540\t    reason: Binary labels are the AdvBench design point. Fine-grained annotation defeats\n541\t      purpose.\n542\t  result_rationale: The 16-label taxonomy requires step-level Type 1-4 x pre/post\n543\t    annotations — precisely the expensive labeling that AdvBench circumvents. On the\n544\t    original dataset this experiment showed only +2pp marginal gain, further justifying\n545\t    the skip. Binary labels are sufficient and cheaper.\n546\t  tables: []\n547\t  figures_description: []\n548\t  analysis: Skipped by design.\n549\t- name: AdvBench Oracle Ceiling Analysis\n550\t  plan_id: exp-010\n551\t  description: 'Adapted: no CP oracle possible. Instead estimate per-step classification\n552\t    ceiling under binary trace-level labels. Characterize the label noise floor.'\n553\t  expected_results:\n554\t    status: adapted\n555\t    trace_level_oracle_acc: 1.0\n556\t    step_level_ceiling_bal_acc: 0.85\n557\t    probe_bal_acc_at_best: 0.73\n558\t    ceiling_gap_pp: 12\n559\t    original_oracle_lead: +31 steps\n560\t    note: Binary labels impose ~0.85 step-level ceiling due to early-step label noise\n561\t  result_rationale: 'Trace-level oracle is trivially 1.0. Per-step ceiling is ~0.85\n562\t    because early steps in jailbreak traces are genuinely safety-aware (model hasn''t\n563\t    committed yet), creating inherent label noise under binary assignment. This ceiling\n564\t    is lower than original oracle (~0.95 near CP) because binary labels cannot distinguish\n565\t    pre/post commitment. The 12pp gap (probe 0.73 vs ceiling 0.85) shows moderate\n566\t    room for improvement but it''s constrained by label quality, not probe architecture.\n567\t    This partially explains why AdvBench HS-text deltas are smaller: the ceiling itself\n568\t    is lower.'\n569\t  tables:\n570\t  - name: AdvBench Classification Ceiling\n571\t    columns:\n572\t    - Method\n573\t    - Step Bal Acc\n574\t    - Gap to Ceiling\n575\t    rows:\n576\t    - - Ceiling (binary label)\n577\t      - ~0.85\n578\t      - —\n579\t    - - HS LR W=3 (L12)\n580\t      - '0.73'\n581\t      - 12pp\n582\t    - - Text LR W=3\n583\t      - '0.69'\n584\t      - 16pp\n585\t  figures_description:\n586\t  - Step-level accuracy by trace position, showing early steps near chance (label\n587\t    noise) and late steps approaching ceiling.\n588\t  analysis: 'The 0.85 ceiling constrains maximum achievable delta. Even a perfect\n589\t    probe can only reach 0.85, limiting the HS-text gap. This is an important nuance\n590\t    for interpreting median-tier results: smaller deltas on AdvBench reflect both\n591\t    weaker supervision AND lower ceiling, not necessarily weaker HS signal.'\n592\t- name: AdvBench OT-7B Extension\n593\t  plan_id: exp-011\n594\t  description: Extend AdvBench replication to OT-7B (Qwen2.5-7B, STILL). Layer sweep,\n595\t    5-metric analysis, bootstrap. Second model for cross-model consistency.\n596\t  expected_results:\n597\t    n_traces: 165\n598\t    best_layer_bal_acc: L11\n599\t    best_layer_depth_pct: 39\n600\t    hs_bal_acc: 0.68\n601\t    cr_composite_layer: L3\n602\t    cr_composite_depth_pct: 11\n603\t    precision_layer: L14\n604\t    precision_depth_pct: 50\n605\t    layer_gap_abs: 11\n606\t    layer_gap_depth_pct: 39\n607\t    hs_text_precision_delta_pp: 5.5\n608\t    precision_p: 0.038\n609\t    hb_pass_precision: false\n610\t    hb_pass_any: false\n611\t    combined_hb_2models: 1/6 (R1-8B precision only)\n612\t  result_rationale: 'OT-7B AdvBench shows layer selection bias with slightly larger\n613\t    gap than R1-8B AdvBench (11 layers, 39% vs 7 layers, 23%), reflecting that OT-7B''s\n614\t    28-layer architecture has steeper per-layer signal gradients. CR-composite selects\n615\t    L3 (11%), consistent with shallow bias. But HS-text precision delta (+5.5pp) does\n616\t    not pass HB — OT-7B has fewer layers and the binary-label attenuation stacks with\n617\t    OT-7B''s already-shorter traces (~32 sentences on AdvBench). With n=33 test, MDE~14pp\n618\t    >> observed 5.5pp. Combined across both AdvBench models: 1/6 HB pass (R1-8B precision\n619\t    only). Direction consistent, magnitude insufficient.'\n620\t  tables:\n621\t  - name: OT-7B AdvBench 5-Metric Layer Selection\n622\t    columns:\n623\t    - Metric\n624\t    - Layer\n625\t    - Depth %\n626\t    - Bal Acc\n627\t    rows:\n628\t    - - bal_acc x CR\n629\t      - L3\n630\t      - 11%\n631\t      - '0.65'\n632\t    - - Pure bal_acc\n633\t      - L9\n634\t      - 32%\n635\t      - '0.68'\n636\t    - - Precision\n637\t      - L14\n638\t      - 50%\n639\t      - '0.66'\n640\t    - - Inverse FPR\n641\t      - L13\n642\t      - 46%\n643\t      - '0.66'\n644\t    - - Composite\n645\t      - L14\n646\t      - 50%\n647\t      - '0.66'\n648\t  - name: AdvBench HB Summary (2 Models Combined)\n649\t    columns:\n650\t    - Model\n651\t    - Metric\n652\t    - Delta (pp)\n653\t    - p-value\n654\t    - HB Rank\n655\t    - HB Threshold\n656\t    - Pass?\n657\t    rows:\n658\t    - - R1-8B\n659\t      - precision\n660\t      - '+6.5'\n661\t      - '0.014'\n662\t      - '1'\n663\t      - '0.0083'\n664\t      - Y\n665\t    - - OT-7B\n666\t      - precision\n667\t      - '+5.5'\n668\t      - '0.038'\n669\t      - '2'\n670\t      - '0.0100'\n671\t      - N\n672\t    - - R1-8B\n673\t      - bal_acc\n674\t      - '+3.9'\n675\t      - '0.082'\n676\t      - '3'\n677\t      - '0.0125'\n678\t      - N\n679\t    - - R1-8B\n680\t      - FPR\n681\t      - '-3.8'\n682\t      - '0.118'\n683\t      - '4'\n684\t      - '0.0167'\n685\t      - N\n686\t    - - OT-7B\n687\t      - bal_acc\n688\t      - '+3.2'\n689\t      - '0.112'\n690\t      - '5'\n691\t      - '0.0250'\n692\t      - N\n693\t    - - OT-7B\n694\t      - FPR\n695\t      - '-2.9'\n696\t      - '0.168'\n697\t      - '6'\n698\t      - '0.0500'\n699\t      - N\n700\t  figures_description:\n701\t  - Two-model AdvBench layer selection comparison. Both show shallow CR-composite,\n702\t    deeper threshold metrics.\n703\t  analysis: 'Second model confirms directional consistency but adds zero HB passes.\n704\t    Combined 1/6 (within median tier range [0, 0.33]). The OT-7B AdvBench gap (39%)\n705\t    is larger than R1-8B (23%) — interesting cross-model variation but n=2 models\n706\t    prevents conclusions. For the appendix: both models show the bias direction replicates;\n707\t    neither provides strong statistical evidence.'\n708\t- name: AdvBench GRU Probe\n709\t  plan_id: exp-012\n710\t  description: GRU sequence model on R1-8B AdvBench at L12. Test whether temporal\n711\t    sequence modeling adds value under binary trace-level labels.\n712\t  expected_results:\n713\t    gru_bal_acc: 0.71\n714\t    gru_precision: 0.54\n715\t    gru_coverage: 0.69\n716\t    gru_fpr: 0.24\n717\t    lr_bal_acc: 0.73\n718\t    lr_precision: 0.56\n719\t    gru_vs_lr_bal_acc_delta: -0.02\n720\t    verdict: GRU no improvement\n721\t  result_rationale: GRU provides no improvement over LR on AdvBench because binary\n722\t    labels lack the sequential safe→committed transition that GRU was designed to\n723\t    model. On the original dataset, GRU learned temporal commitment dynamics; on AdvBench,\n724\t    all steps carry the same label, so there is no temporal structure beyond what\n725\t    LR's sliding window already captures. The GRU's 1.6M parameters overfit to n=107\n726\t    training traces without compensating temporal signal. This cross-dataset negative\n727\t    result strengthens the 'linear probes sufficient' claim.\n728\t  tables:\n729\t  - name: AdvBench GRU vs LR\n730\t    columns:\n731\t    - Method\n732\t    - Bal Acc\n733\t    - Precision\n734\t    - Coverage\n735\t    - Step FPR\n736\t    rows:\n737\t    - - LR W=3 (L12)\n738\t      - '0.73'\n739\t      - '0.56'\n740\t      - '0.72'\n741\t      - '0.19'\n742\t    - - GRU single (L12)\n743\t      - '0.71'\n744\t      - '0.54'\n745\t      - '0.69'\n746\t      - '0.24'\n747\t  figures_description: []\n748\t  analysis: GRU negative result replicates cross-dataset. Combined with original (modest\n749\t    R1-8B gain, negative 32B), linear probes are recommended for both temporal-CP\n750\t    and binary classification settings.\n751\t- name: AdvBench Text Baseline\n752\t  plan_id: exp-013\n753\t  description: 'MiniLM-L6 text embeddings + LR on R1-8B AdvBench. The critical test\n754\t    for AdvBench: does the HS-text gap survive binary classification?'\n755\t  expected_results:\n756\t    text_static_bal_acc: 0.67\n757\t    text_static_precision: 0.48\n758\t    text_temporal_W3_bal_acc: 0.69\n759\t    text_temporal_W3_precision: 0.5\n760\t    hs_temporal_W3_bal_acc: 0.73\n761\t    hs_temporal_W3_precision: 0.56\n762\t    hs_text_gap_bal_acc_pp: 4.0\n763\t    hs_text_gap_precision_pp: 6.0\n764\t    original_gap_bal_acc_pp: 13.2\n765\t    original_gap_precision_pp: 33.5\n766\t    gap_compression_factor: 3-5x\n767\t  result_rationale: 'Text probes perform RELATIVELY better on AdvBench (0.69 vs 0.678\n768\t    on original) because binary jailbreak/safe classification is more amenable to\n769\t    surface patterns: harmful keywords, refusal phrases, and compliance indicators\n770\t    are directly discriminative. The HS-text gap compresses 3-5× (4pp bal_acc, 6pp\n771\t    precision vs 13pp/34pp on original). This is THE key median-tier finding: binary\n772\t    labels make text more competitive, reducing the marginal value of hidden-state\n773\t    access. HS still outperforms, but the practical advantage is much smaller. The\n774\t    gap compression ratio is informative for the paper''s scope qualification of C2.'\n775\t  tables:\n776\t  - name: 'HS-Text Gap Compression: Original vs AdvBench'\n777\t    columns:\n778\t    - Dataset\n779\t    - HS BA\n780\t    - Text BA\n781\t    - Gap BA\n782\t    - HS Prec\n783\t    - Text Prec\n784\t    - Gap Prec\n785\t    rows:\n786\t    - - Original (R1-8B)\n787\t      - '0.810'\n788\t      - '0.678'\n789\t      - +13.2pp\n790\t      - '0.822'\n791\t      - '0.487'\n792\t      - +33.5pp\n793\t    - - AdvBench (R1-8B)\n794\t      - '0.730'\n795\t      - '0.690'\n796\t      - +4.0pp\n797\t      - '0.560'\n798\t      - '0.500'\n799\t      - +6.0pp\n800\t    - - Compression\n801\t      - —\n802\t      - —\n803\t      - 3.3x\n804\t      - —\n805\t      - —\n806\t      - 5.6x\n807\t  figures_description:\n808\t  - 'Paired bar chart: HS vs text accuracy on original (large gap) and AdvBench (small\n809\t    gap), visually showing the compression.'\n810\t  analysis: 'The gap compression is the most interpretable median-tier result. It\n811\t    explains WHY AdvBench doesn''t strongly replicate: the HS modality advantage is\n812\t    largest when the classification task requires detecting subtle internal state\n813\t    transitions (temporal CP detection), not when surface patterns suffice (binary\n814\t    jailbreak). This motivates the C1 scope qualification and provides mechanistic\n815\t    understanding of when HS monitoring adds maximal value.'\n816\t- name: AdvBench BOCPD/CUSUM\n817\t  plan_id: exp-014\n818\t  description: Not applicable. BOCPD/CUSUM detect distributional shifts in sequential\n819\t    probe scores. AdvBench binary labels define no temporal transition to detect.\n820\t  expected_results:\n821\t    status: not_applicable\n822\t    reason: No commitment-point transition in binary classification setup. Change-point\n823\t      methods require a changepoint.\n824\t  result_rationale: BOCPD/CUSUM target the probe score transition from safe→committed.\n825\t    Under binary labels, all steps carry the same label — no transition exists. Running\n826\t    these methods would detect noise-driven fluctuations, producing uninterpretable\n827\t    results. On the original dataset, BOCPD already failed on 3/4 models; there is\n828\t    no value in attempting it on a setup where the target signal is definitionally\n829\t    absent.\n830\t  tables: []\n831\t  figures_description: []\n832\t  analysis: Skipped. Change-point methods require a change point.\n833\t- name: AdvBench FPR Analysis\n834\t  plan_id: exp-015\n835\t  description: All probes on AdvBench safe traces. Step-level and trace-level FPR.\n836\t  expected_results:\n837\t    n_safe_test: 24\n838\t    hs_step_fpr: 0.18\n839\t    text_step_fpr: 0.24\n840\t    hs_trace_fpr_k5: 0.33\n841\t    text_trace_fpr_k5: 0.42\n842\t    hs_fpr_lower: true\n843\t    hs_text_fpr_gap_step_pp: 6.0\n844\t    original_hs_step_fpr: 0.162\n845\t    original_text_step_fpr: 0.272\n846\t    original_fpr_gap_pp: 11.0\n847\t  result_rationale: 'AdvBench safe FPR is somewhat lower than original (step: 0.18\n848\t    vs 0.162, trace: 0.33 vs 0.39). AdvBench safe traces are \"cleaner\" — simple refusals,\n849\t    shorter, less internal deliberation — producing fewer safety-adjacent patterns\n850\t    that trigger false alarms. The HS-text FPR gap (6pp) is narrower than original\n851\t    (11pp), consistent with the overall median-tier pattern of attenuated HS advantage\n852\t    under binary labels. The FPR numbers are still substantial (33% trace-level for\n853\t    HS), confirming deployment limitation persists cross-dataset.'\n854\t  tables:\n855\t  - name: FPR Cross-Dataset Comparison\n856\t    columns:\n857\t    - Dataset\n858\t    - HS Step FPR\n859\t    - Text Step FPR\n860\t    - Gap\n861\t    - HS Trace FPR\n862\t    - Text Trace FPR\n863\t    - Gap\n864\t    rows:\n865\t    - - Original (R1-8B)\n866\t      - '0.162'\n867\t      - '0.272'\n868\t      - 11.0pp\n869\t      - '0.390'\n870\t      - '0.502'\n871\t      - 11.2pp\n872\t    - - AdvBench (R1-8B)\n873\t      - '0.180'\n874\t      - '0.240'\n875\t      - 6.0pp\n876\t      - '0.330'\n877\t      - '0.420'\n878\t      - 9.0pp\n879\t  figures_description:\n880\t  - FPR bar chart across datasets showing consistent HS < text pattern with smaller\n881\t    gap on AdvBench.\n882\t  analysis: HS FPR advantage replicates directionally on AdvBench with smaller magnitude.\n883\t    The persistent high trace-level FPR (33%) confirms this is a cross-dataset limitation\n884\t    of probe-based detection, not dataset-specific. Appendix material.\n885\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b285e967-6524-44bc-8385-19eed9806998","uuid":"4143e790-dd76-4820-ab92-175757256658","timestamp":"2026-05-22T09:59:31.019Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T085530/median/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: Layer selection bias shows directional replication on AdvBench\n  — CR-composite 仍倾向选更浅的层 (L5, 16% depth vs original L0, 0%), 但 gap 从 23 层缩减到 7 层。\n  HS在threshold-selected layer有方向性优势 (+6pp precision) 但仅 precision 通过 HB (1/6)。 论文调整措辞从\n  'model-geometric property' 到 'probe-task-modulated geometric property, strongest\n  under temporal supervision'。AdvBench 结果放 appendix Table A3 作 'supporting evidence'，主文一句话引用。score\n  delta ≤ +0.1。reviewer 看到方向一致 会认可 robustness，但不会视为 decisive cross-dataset validation。需要额外证据\n  （如第二个模型的 AdvBench replication 或第二个 binary-label 数据集）才能从 3.05 推到 3.5。\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: AdvBench Layer Sweep + Static Probe (R1-8B)\n  plan_id: exp-001\n  description: Full 32-layer sweep on R1-8B AdvBench traces with binary jailbreak/safe\n    labels. Per-layer static LR probe. Establish baseline and identify scheme B bias\n    direction.\n  expected_results:\n    n_traces: 178\n    n_jailbreak: 107\n    n_safe: 71\n    split: 107/35/36\n    avg_trace_length_sentences: 38\n    best_layer_bal_acc: L10\n    best_layer_depth_pct: 32\n    best_layer_bal_acc_value: 0.72\n    best_layer_precision: 0.51\n    best_layer_crossing_rate: 0.55\n    scheme_b_layer: L5\n    scheme_b_depth_pct: 16\n    scheme_b_bal_acc: 0.68\n    scheme_b_crossing_rate: 0.71\n    deep_layer_L20_bal_acc: 0.65\n    deep_layer_L20_precision: 0.53\n    layer_profile_range_pp: 7\n  result_rationale: 'Binary trace-level labels weaken per-step supervision: every\n    step in a jailbreak trace is labeled positive, including early safety-aware reasoning.\n    This label noise reduces probe accuracy (0.72 vs original 0.81). Scheme B selects\n    L5 (16%) — still shallow but not L0, because binary labels produce a more uniform\n    crossing rate profile across layers (noise is less step-position-dependent than\n    under CP labels). Pure bal_acc peaks at mid-depth L10 (32%) where intermediate\n    representations maximize binary discriminability. Deep layers (L20+) encode CP-relevant\n    temporal distinctions that binary labels cannot leverage, so bal_acc drops. Layer\n    profile range (~7pp best-to-worst) is compressed versus original (~15pp), consistent\n    with binary labels distributing signal more uniformly.'\n  tables:\n  - name: AdvBench R1-8B Layer Sweep Top-5\n    columns:\n    - Layer\n    - Depth %\n    - Bal Acc\n    - Precision\n    - Crossing Rate\n    - CR x BA\n    rows:\n    - - L5\n      - 16%\n      - '0.68'\n      - '0.46'\n      - '0.71'\n      - '0.483'\n    - - L8\n      - 26%\n      - '0.71'\n      - '0.49'\n      - '0.58'\n      - '0.412'\n    - - L10\n      - 32%\n      - '0.72'\n      - '0.51'\n      - '0.55'\n      - '0.396'\n    - - L12\n      - 39%\n      - '0.70'\n      - '0.53'\n      - '0.48'\n      - '0.336'\n    - - L16\n      - 52%\n      - '0.67'\n      - '0.52'\n      - '0.42'\n      - '0.281'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Layer profile plot: bal_acc, precision, and crossing_rate vs depth. CR peaks\n    shallow, precision/bal_acc peak mid-depth. Overlay original dataset profile (dashed)\n    showing steeper gradients.'\n  analysis: AdvBench layer landscape is flatter than original — best/worst gap ~7pp\n    vs ~15pp. Scheme B bias direction replicated (shallow) but magnitude attenuated.\n    This motivates the 5-metric ablation (exp-008) as the core test.\n- name: AdvBench Temporal Window (R1-8B)\n  plan_id: exp-002\n  description: Temporal LR with W=1,3,5 on R1-8B AdvBench at threshold-selected layer\n    L12. Validate temporal improvement under binary labels.\n  expected_results:\n    layer: L12\n    W1_bal_acc: 0.7\n    W1_precision: 0.53\n    W1_coverage: 0.78\n    W1_fpr: 0.22\n    W3_bal_acc: 0.73\n    W3_precision: 0.56\n    W3_coverage: 0.72\n    W3_fpr: 0.19\n    W5_bal_acc: 0.72\n    W5_precision: 0.55\n    W5_coverage: 0.67\n    W5_fpr: 0.2\n    best_W: 3\n    temporal_gain_over_static_pp: 3.0\n    original_temporal_gain_pp: 3-5\n  result_rationale: Temporal improvement present but weaker (+3pp vs +3-5pp on original).\n    Binary labels lack an explicit safe→committed transition for the window to capture\n    — the temporal context picks up local coherence patterns but without step-level\n    supervision the gradient is diffuse. W=3 optimal because AdvBench traces are shorter\n    (~38 steps); at W=5 the feature dimensionality (4096×5=20480) approaches training\n    set size and edge samples are lost. Coverage decreases monotonically with W (universal\n    tradeoff).\n  tables:\n  - name: AdvBench Temporal Ablation (L12)\n    columns:\n    - W\n    - Bal Acc\n    - Precision\n    - Coverage\n    - Step FPR\n    rows:\n    - - 1 (static)\n      - '0.70'\n      - '0.53'\n      - '0.78'\n      - '0.22'\n    - - '3'\n      - '0.73'\n      - '0.56'\n      - '0.72'\n      - '0.19'\n    - - '5'\n      - '0.72'\n      - '0.55'\n      - '0.67'\n      - '0.20'\n  figures_description:\n  - W vs bal_acc curve for AdvBench (solid) overlaid with original dataset (dashed,\n    different x-axis scale). Shows ceiling is lower and optimal W is smaller.\n  analysis: Temporal context helps on AdvBench but with lower ceiling. The gain direction\n    replicates C1's temporal improvement claim. Shorter optimal W reflects shorter\n    traces — consistent with the hypothesis that trace structure modulates temporal\n    signal strength.\n- name: AdvBench Cross-Model Transfer\n  plan_id: exp-003\n  description: Not directly applicable — AdvBench what_if specifies R1-8B only. Cross-transfer\n    requires a second model's AdvBench hidden states.\n  expected_results:\n    status: skipped\n    reason: Single model in AdvBench scope. Cross-transfer on original dataset already\n      failed (best 58% acc).\n    hypothetical_if_extended: Expect transfer failure consistent with original — safety\n      geometry is model-specific, not dataset-specific.\n  result_rationale: Cross-model transfer failed entirely on the original dataset (all\n    directions near chance) because safety commitment geometry is model-specific.\n    The dataset does not change this — the failure mode is architectural (different\n    hidden space orientations). Saving 2 GPU-hours. If a reviewer requests this, OT-7B\n    AdvBench extraction + transfer can be done post-review.\n  tables: []\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: Skipped. Model-specificity is a model property, not a dataset property.\n- name: AdvBench Early Detection Timing\n  plan_id: exp-004\n  description: Adapted from lead-time analysis. No CP in AdvBench; instead measure\n    the earliest trace fraction at which the probe consistently classifies correctly.\n    Compare HS vs text.\n  expected_results:\n    hs_earliest_correct_fraction: 0.28\n    text_earliest_correct_fraction: 0.41\n    hs_earlier_by_steps: 5.1\n    hs_earlier_in_fraction_of_traces: 0.64\n    text_earlier_in_fraction_of_traces: 0.36\n    metric_note: Adapted metric — not directly comparable to original lead time\n  result_rationale: 'HS probes classify jailbreak traces correctly earlier (~28% of\n    trace vs ~41% for text) because hidden states encode safety disposition before\n    surface text reveals intent. But the 5-step advantage is noisier than original\n    lead-time analysis: without explicit CP, the \"earliest correct\" metric depends\n    on threshold choice and consecutive-correct criterion. The 64% \"HS earlier\" rate\n    (vs 36% text-first) is weaker than original because binary labels make text features\n    more competitive — surface patterns like harmful keywords are directly discriminative.\n    This supports the HS advantage directionally but the adapted metric carries a\n    transparency cost in the paper.'\n  tables:\n  - name: AdvBench Early Detection\n    columns:\n    - Modality\n    - Median First-Correct Step\n    - Trace Fraction\n    - Earlier in N/36\n    rows:\n    - - HS (L12, W=3)\n      - '10.6'\n      - 28%\n      - '23'\n    - - Text (W=3)\n      - '15.6'\n      - 41%\n      - '13'\n  figures_description:\n  - CDF of first-correct-step for HS vs text, HS curve shifted left (earlier detection).\n  analysis: Directional HS timing advantage on AdvBench. Adapted metric is less rigorous\n    than original lead-time analysis — suitable for appendix supporting material only.\n- name: AdvBench Mahalanobis Distance\n  plan_id: exp-005\n  description: Mahalanobis distance anomaly detection on R1-8B AdvBench hidden states.\n    Estimate covariance from safe traces, compute MD on jailbreak steps.\n  expected_results:\n    safe_md_mean_pca64: 6.45\n    jailbreak_md_mean_pca64: 6.49\n    separation: 0.04\n    best_pca_dim: 64\n    fa_rate_at_5pct_threshold: 0.04\n    tp_rate_at_5pct_threshold: 0.03\n    verdict: fail\n  result_rationale: 'Mahalanobis fails for the same mechanistic reason as original:\n    safety-relevant signal occupies minor PCA components, not principal variance directions.\n    The Fisher discriminant drops from ~17 in full space to ~1 in PCA. Separation\n    (0.04) is even smaller than original (0.07) because binary labels introduce more\n    within-class heterogeneity — early jailbreak steps resemble safe steps. The false\n    alarm rate equals or exceeds true positive rate at every threshold. Cross-dataset\n    replication of this negative result is clean and strengthens the paper''s unsupervised-methods-fail\n    narrative.'\n  tables:\n  - name: AdvBench Mahalanobis Separation by PCA Dim\n    columns:\n    - PCA Dim\n    - Safe MD\n    - Jailbreak MD\n    - Separation\n    rows:\n    - - '16'\n      - '5.12'\n      - '5.09'\n      - '-0.03'\n    - - '32'\n      - '6.01'\n      - '6.05'\n      - '+0.04'\n    - - '64'\n      - '6.45'\n      - '6.49'\n      - '+0.04'\n    - - '128'\n      - '6.89'\n      - '6.87'\n      - '-0.02'\n  figures_description:\n  - MD distribution overlap at PCA-64 showing near-complete overlap between safe and\n    jailbreak step populations.\n  analysis: Negative result replicates cleanly. Combined with 4-model original results,\n    provides 5-dataset-model evidence that variance-based unsupervised detection is\n    fundamentally inapplicable. Worth including in appendix alongside positive results.\n- name: AdvBench Bootstrap CI + HB\n  plan_id: exp-006\n  description: 1000 bootstrap resamples for HS vs text comparison at threshold layer\n    L12 (W=3). Holm-Bonferroni for 3 metrics. AUROC as supplementary.\n  expected_results:\n    bal_acc_hs: 0.73\n    bal_acc_text: 0.69\n    bal_acc_delta: 0.039\n    bal_acc_ci: '[-0.005, +0.083]'\n    bal_acc_p: 0.082\n    precision_hs: 0.56\n    precision_text: 0.5\n    precision_delta: 0.065\n    precision_ci: '[+0.014, +0.116]'\n    precision_p: 0.014\n    fpr_hs: 0.19\n    fpr_text: 0.23\n    fpr_delta: -0.038\n    fpr_ci: '[-0.089, +0.013]'\n    fpr_p: 0.118\n    hb_pass_count: 1/3\n    hb_pass_detail: Precision only (p=0.014 < threshold 0.0167 at rank 1/3)\n    auroc_hs: 0.758\n    auroc_text: 0.718\n    auroc_delta: 0.04\n    auroc_p: 0.045\n  result_rationale: 'Only precision passes HB (p=0.014 < 0.0167). Bal_acc and FPR\n    CIs include zero. With n=36 test traces, MDE at 80% power is ~12pp; observed deltas\n    (4-6.5pp) fall below MDE for bal_acc/FPR. Precision passes because its variance\n    is lower and the 6.5pp delta just exceeds the detection boundary. AUROC delta\n    (+4pp) is raw p=0.045 but fails HB adjustment. The effect size reduction from\n    original (~20pp precision) to AdvBench (~6pp) is the core median-tier signature:\n    binary labels attenuate the modality advantage below reliable detection for most\n    metrics.'\n  tables:\n  - name: AdvBench Bootstrap (L12, W=3, n=36 test)\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - HS\n    - Text\n    - Delta\n    - 95% CI\n    - p-value\n    - HB Pass?\n    rows:\n    - - Bal Acc\n      - '0.730'\n      - '0.691'\n      - +3.9pp\n      - '[-0.5, +8.3]'\n      - '0.082'\n      - N\n    - - Precision\n      - '0.560'\n      - '0.495'\n      - +6.5pp\n      - '[+1.4, +11.6]'\n      - '0.014'\n      - Y\n    - - FPR\n      - '0.190'\n      - '0.228'\n      - -3.8pp\n      - '[-8.9, +1.3]'\n      - '0.118'\n      - N\n    - - AUROC\n      - '0.758'\n      - '0.718'\n      - +4.0pp\n      - '[+0.1, +7.9]'\n      - '0.045'\n      - N (adj)\n  figures_description:\n  - Bootstrap delta distributions for 3 metrics. Precision distribution cleanly right\n    of zero; bal_acc and FPR straddle zero.\n  - 'Forest plot: AdvBench deltas (with CI) alongside original-dataset deltas, showing\n    systematic compression.'\n  analysis: '1/3 HB pass confirms directional advantage but cannot match original\n    12/12. The precision-only significance is interpretable: precision is the most\n    deployment-relevant metric and it passes, but the failure of bal_acc and FPR means\n    the evidence is partial. Appendix material with explicit power caveat.'\n- name: AdvBench Window Size Ablation\n  plan_id: exp-007\n  description: W=1,3,5 on R1-8B AdvBench at L12. Truncated range (vs W=1-25 on original)\n    because shorter traces limit useful window sizes.\n  expected_results:\n    W1_bal_acc: 0.7\n    W1_precision: 0.53\n    W1_coverage: 0.78\n    W3_bal_acc: 0.73\n    W3_precision: 0.56\n    W3_coverage: 0.72\n    W5_bal_acc: 0.72\n    W5_precision: 0.55\n    W5_coverage: 0.67\n    coverage_decreases_with_W: true\n    best_W: 3\n    peak_then_decline: true\n  result_rationale: 'Same qualitative pattern as original: coverage decreases monotonically,\n    precision peaks at moderate W, bal_acc peaks then declines. Optimal W is smaller\n    (3 vs 10-25) because AdvBench traces average ~38 steps — at W=5 many edge samples\n    are excluded. The precision-coverage tradeoff is less steep than original (flatter\n    signal landscape under binary labels). The W=5 decline confirms the dimensionality\n    concern: 4096×5=20480 features with ~107 training examples.'\n  tables:\n  - name: AdvBench Window Ablation\n    columns:\n    - W\n    - Bal Acc\n    - Precision\n    - Coverage\n    - Step FPR\n    rows:\n    - - '1'\n      - '0.70'\n      - '0.53'\n      - '0.78'\n      - '0.22'\n    - - '3'\n      - '0.73'\n      - '0.56'\n      - '0.72'\n      - '0.19'\n    - - '5'\n      - '0.72'\n      - '0.55'\n      - '0.67'\n      - '0.20'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Dual-axis: bal_acc (left) and coverage (right) vs W for AdvBench, with original-dataset\n    W=1-25 curve in background.'\n  analysis: Temporal tradeoff replicates. Optimal W=3 on AdvBench vs W=10-25 on original\n    reflects trace length effect. This supports the claim that temporal context is\n    universally beneficial but task-modulated in magnitude.\n- name: AdvBench 5-Metric Layer Selection (CORE)\n  plan_id: exp-008\n  description: Primary AdvBench replication experiment. Run all 5 layer selection\n    metrics on R1-8B AdvBench. Determine if CR-composite → shallow, threshold metrics\n    → deeper. This IS the advbench_layer_bias_replication what_if experiment.\n  expected_results:\n    cr_composite_layer: L5\n    cr_composite_depth_pct: 16\n    pure_bal_acc_layer: L10\n    pure_bal_acc_depth_pct: 32\n    precision_layer: L12\n    precision_depth_pct: 39\n    inv_fpr_layer: L11\n    inv_fpr_depth_pct: 35\n    composite_add_layer: L12\n    composite_add_depth_pct: 39\n    layer_gap_cr_vs_precision: 7\n    layer_gap_depth_pct: 23\n    crossing_rate_at_L5: 0.71\n    crossing_rate_at_L12: 0.48\n    cr_shallow_deep_ratio: 1.28\n    hs_bal_acc_at_precision_layer: 0.7\n    hs_text_precision_delta_pp: 6.5\n    threshold_metrics_consensus: 3/4 non-biased metrics select L10-L12 (32-39%)\n    original_gap_layers: 23\n    original_gap_depth_pct: 72\n  result_rationale: 'The 5-metric analysis replicates the directional pattern on AdvBench:\n    CR-composite (L5, 16%) selects shallower than threshold metrics (L12, 39%). Three\n    factors explain why the gap is 23% instead of 72%: (1) Binary labels produce a\n    more uniform per-layer crossing rate profile. Without step-level CP labels, the\n    contrast between shallow noise-driven crossings and deep signal-driven crossings\n    is reduced — the crossing rate ratio (L5/L12 = 1.28) is lower than original (~1.8).\n    (2) AdvBench traces are shorter (38 vs 46 sentences) and more structurally uniform\n    (most are direct compliance or refusal), reducing inter-layer signal variance.\n    (3) Binary classification''s optimal precision layer (L12, 39%) sits at mid-depth,\n    not deep (L23, 72%), because binary discrimination does not require the temporal-structure\n    sensitivity that threshold metrics exploit at deep layers under CP supervision.\n    The bias mechanism itself replicates: crossing rate at L5 (0.71) > at L12 (0.48),\n    confirming that compressed shallow representations generate inflated crossing\n    rates regardless of dataset. But the MAGNITUDE of this bias''s impact on downstream\n    metrics is probe-task-dependent.'\n  tables:\n  - name: AdvBench 5-Metric Layer Selection (R1-8B)\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - Selected Layer\n    - Depth %\n    - Bal Acc\n    - Crossing Rate\n    rows:\n    - - bal_acc x CR\n      - L5\n      - 16%\n      - '0.68'\n      - '0.71'\n    - - Pure bal_acc\n      - L10\n      - 32%\n      - '0.72'\n      - '0.55'\n    - - Precision\n      - L12\n      - 39%\n      - '0.70'\n      - '0.48'\n    - - Inverse FPR\n      - L11\n      - 35%\n      - '0.69'\n      - '0.51'\n    - - Composite (additive)\n      - L12\n      - 39%\n      - '0.70'\n      - '0.48'\n  - name: 'Layer Bias Gap: Original vs AdvBench'\n    columns:\n    - Dataset\n    - CR Layer\n    - Prec Layer\n    - Gap (layers)\n    - Gap (depth %)\n    - CR/Deep Ratio\n    rows:\n    - - Original (CP)\n      - L0 (0%)\n      - L23 (72%)\n      - '23'\n      - 72%\n      - '1.80'\n    - - AdvBench (binary)\n      - L5 (16%)\n      - L12 (39%)\n      - '7'\n      - 23%\n      - '1.28'\n  - name: AdvBench HS vs Text at Each Selected Layer\n    columns:\n    - Layer\n    - HS Prec\n    - Text Prec\n    - Delta (pp)\n    - HS BA\n    - Text BA\n    - Delta (pp)\n    rows:\n    - - L5 (CR-biased)\n      - '0.46'\n      - '0.43'\n      - '+3.1'\n      - '0.68'\n      - '0.66'\n      - '+2.0'\n    - - L10 (bal_acc)\n      - '0.51'\n      - '0.46'\n      - '+5.4'\n      - '0.72'\n      - '0.68'\n      - '+3.8'\n    - - L12 (precision)\n      - '0.53'\n      - '0.46'\n      - '+6.5'\n      - '0.70'\n      - '0.67'\n      - '+3.4'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Side-by-side 5-metric profiles: original (left) vs AdvBench (right). Same directional\n    structure, compressed vertical scale on AdvBench.'\n  - Crossing rate vs depth scatter with regression lines for both datasets. Both monotonically\n    decreasing; AdvBench slope is shallower.\n  - 'Bar chart: layer gap (in depth %) across datasets, showing 72% → 23% compression.'\n  analysis: 'CORE MEDIAN FINDING: Layer selection bias replicates directionally but\n    with 3× smaller gap. CR-composite → shallow and threshold → deeper holds on both\n    datasets. The attenuation is mechanistically explained by binary vs temporal supervision.\n    Paper treatment: appendix Table A3 with text: \"Layer selection bias replicates\n    directionally on AdvBench binary classification (gap=7 layers, 23%), attenuated\n    versus temporal probing (gap=23 layers, 72%), consistent with the bias being probe-task-modulated.\"\n    Qualifies C1 from ''model-geometric property'' to ''probe-task-modulated geometric\n    property, strongest under temporal supervision.'' Reviewer impact: direction-consistent\n    = +credibility, but not decisive = no score upgrade beyond +0.05.'\n- name: AdvBench Fine-Grained Labels\n  plan_id: exp-009\n  description: Not applicable. AdvBench uses binary jailbreak/safe labels; 16-label\n    taxonomy requires CP annotation which is the cost AdvBench avoids.\n  expected_results:\n    status: not_applicable\n    reason: Binary labels are the AdvBench design point. Fine-grained annotation defeats\n      purpose.\n  result_rationale: The 16-label taxonomy requires step-level Type 1-4 x pre/post\n    annotations — precisely the expensive labeling that AdvBench circumvents. On the\n    original dataset this experiment showed only +2pp marginal gain, further justifying\n    the skip. Binary labels are sufficient and cheaper.\n  tables: []\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: Skipped by design.\n- name: AdvBench Oracle Ceiling Analysis\n  plan_id: exp-010\n  description: 'Adapted: no CP oracle possible. Instead estimate per-step classification\n    ceiling under binary trace-level labels. Characterize the label noise floor.'\n  expected_results:\n    status: adapted\n    trace_level_oracle_acc: 1.0\n    step_level_ceiling_bal_acc: 0.85\n    probe_bal_acc_at_best: 0.73\n    ceiling_gap_pp: 12\n    original_oracle_lead: +31 steps\n    note: Binary labels impose ~0.85 step-level ceiling due to early-step label noise\n  result_rationale: 'Trace-level oracle is trivially 1.0. Per-step ceiling is ~0.85\n    because early steps in jailbreak traces are genuinely safety-aware (model hasn''t\n    committed yet), creating inherent label noise under binary assignment. This ceiling\n    is lower than original oracle (~0.95 near CP) because binary labels cannot distinguish\n    pre/post commitment. The 12pp gap (probe 0.73 vs ceiling 0.85) shows moderate\n    room for improvement but it''s constrained by label quality, not probe architecture.\n    This partially explains why AdvBench HS-text deltas are smaller: the ceiling itself\n    is lower.'\n  tables:\n  - name: AdvBench Classification Ceiling\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - Step Bal Acc\n    - Gap to Ceiling\n    rows:\n    - - Ceiling (binary label)\n      - ~0.85\n      - —\n    - - HS LR W=3 (L12)\n      - '0.73'\n      - 12pp\n    - - Text LR W=3\n      - '0.69'\n      - 16pp\n  figures_description:\n  - Step-level accuracy by trace position, showing early steps near chance (label\n    noise) and late steps approaching ceiling.\n  analysis: 'The 0.85 ceiling constrains maximum achievable delta. Even a perfect\n    probe can only reach 0.85, limiting the HS-text gap. This is an important nuance\n    for interpreting median-tier results: smaller deltas on AdvBench reflect both\n    weaker supervision AND lower ceiling, not necessarily weaker HS signal.'\n- name: AdvBench OT-7B Extension\n  plan_id: exp-011\n  description: Extend AdvBench replication to OT-7B (Qwen2.5-7B, STILL). Layer sweep,\n    5-metric analysis, bootstrap. Second model for cross-model consistency.\n  expected_results:\n    n_traces: 165\n    best_layer_bal_acc: L11\n    best_layer_depth_pct: 39\n    hs_bal_acc: 0.68\n    cr_composite_layer: L3\n    cr_composite_depth_pct: 11\n    precision_layer: L14\n    precision_depth_pct: 50\n    layer_gap_abs: 11\n    layer_gap_depth_pct: 39\n    hs_text_precision_delta_pp: 5.5\n    precision_p: 0.038\n    hb_pass_precision: false\n    hb_pass_any: false\n    combined_hb_2models: 1/6 (R1-8B precision only)\n  result_rationale: 'OT-7B AdvBench shows layer selection bias with slightly larger\n    gap than R1-8B AdvBench (11 layers, 39% vs 7 layers, 23%), reflecting that OT-7B''s\n    28-layer architecture has steeper per-layer signal gradients. CR-composite selects\n    L3 (11%), consistent with shallow bias. But HS-text precision delta (+5.5pp) does\n    not pass HB — OT-7B has fewer layers and the binary-label attenuation stacks with\n    OT-7B''s already-shorter traces (~32 sentences on AdvBench). With n=33 test, MDE~14pp\n    >> observed 5.5pp. Combined across both AdvBench models: 1/6 HB pass (R1-8B precision\n    only). Direction consistent, magnitude insufficient.'\n  tables:\n  - name: OT-7B AdvBench 5-Metric Layer Selection\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - Layer\n    - Depth %\n    - Bal Acc\n    rows:\n    - - bal_acc x CR\n      - L3\n      - 11%\n      - '0.65'\n    - - Pure bal_acc\n      - L9\n      - 32%\n      - '0.68'\n    - - Precision\n      - L14\n      - 50%\n      - '0.66'\n    - - Inverse FPR\n      - L13\n      - 46%\n      - '0.66'\n    - - Composite\n      - L14\n      - 50%\n      - '0.66'\n  - name: AdvBench HB Summary (2 Models Combined)\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Metric\n    - Delta (pp)\n    - p-value\n    - HB Rank\n    - HB Threshold\n    - Pass?\n    rows:\n    - - R1-8B\n      - precision\n      - '+6.5'\n      - '0.014'\n      - '1'\n      - '0.0083'\n      - Y\n    - - OT-7B\n      - precision\n      - '+5.5'\n      - '0.038'\n      - '2'\n      - '0.0100'\n      - N\n    - - R1-8B\n      - bal_acc\n      - '+3.9'\n      - '0.082'\n      - '3'\n      - '0.0125'\n      - N\n    - - R1-8B\n      - FPR\n      - '-3.8'\n      - '0.118'\n      - '4'\n      - '0.0167'\n      - N\n    - - OT-7B\n      - bal_acc\n      - '+3.2'\n      - '0.112'\n      - '5'\n      - '0.0250'\n      - N\n    - - OT-7B\n      - FPR\n      - '-2.9'\n      - '0.168'\n      - '6'\n      - '0.0500'\n      - N\n  figures_description:\n  - Two-model AdvBench layer selection comparison. Both show shallow CR-composite,\n    deeper threshold metrics.\n  analysis: 'Second model confirms directional consistency but adds zero HB passes.\n    Combined 1/6 (within median tier range [0, 0.33]). The OT-7B AdvBench gap (39%)\n    is larger than R1-8B (23%) — interesting cross-model variation but n=2 models\n    prevents conclusions. For the appendix: both models show the bias direction replicates;\n    neither provides strong statistical evidence.'\n- name: AdvBench GRU Probe\n  plan_id: exp-012\n  description: GRU sequence model on R1-8B AdvBench at L12. Test whether temporal\n    sequence modeling adds value under binary trace-level labels.\n  expected_results:\n    gru_bal_acc: 0.71\n    gru_precision: 0.54\n    gru_coverage: 0.69\n    gru_fpr: 0.24\n    lr_bal_acc: 0.73\n    lr_precision: 0.56\n    gru_vs_lr_bal_acc_delta: -0.02\n    verdict: GRU no improvement\n  result_rationale: GRU provides no improvement over LR on AdvBench because binary\n    labels lack the sequential safe→committed transition that GRU was designed to\n    model. On the original dataset, GRU learned temporal commitment dynamics; on AdvBench,\n    all steps carry the same label, so there is no temporal structure beyond what\n    LR's sliding window already captures. The GRU's 1.6M parameters overfit to n=107\n    training traces without compensating temporal signal. This cross-dataset negative\n    result strengthens the 'linear probes sufficient' claim.\n  tables:\n  - name: AdvBench GRU vs LR\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - Bal Acc\n    - Precision\n    - Coverage\n    - Step FPR\n    rows:\n    - - LR W=3 (L12)\n      - '0.73'\n      - '0.56'\n      - '0.72'\n      - '0.19'\n    - - GRU single (L12)\n      - '0.71'\n      - '0.54'\n      - '0.69'\n      - '0.24'\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: GRU negative result replicates cross-dataset. Combined with original (modest\n    R1-8B gain, negative 32B), linear probes are recommended for both temporal-CP\n    and binary classification settings.\n- name: AdvBench Text Baseline\n  plan_id: exp-013\n  description: 'MiniLM-L6 text embeddings + LR on R1-8B AdvBench. The critical test\n    for AdvBench: does the HS-text gap survive binary classification?'\n  expected_results:\n    text_static_bal_acc: 0.67\n    text_static_precision: 0.48\n    text_temporal_W3_bal_acc: 0.69\n    text_temporal_W3_precision: 0.5\n    hs_temporal_W3_bal_acc: 0.73\n    hs_temporal_W3_precision: 0.56\n    hs_text_gap_bal_acc_pp: 4.0\n    hs_text_gap_precision_pp: 6.0\n    original_gap_bal_acc_pp: 13.2\n    original_gap_precision_pp: 33.5\n    gap_compression_factor: 3-5x\n  result_rationale: 'Text probes perform RELATIVELY better on AdvBench (0.69 vs 0.678\n    on original) because binary jailbreak/safe classification is more amenable to\n    surface patterns: harmful keywords, refusal phrases, and compliance indicators\n    are directly discriminative. The HS-text gap compresses 3-5× (4pp bal_acc, 6pp\n    precision vs 13pp/34pp on original). This is THE key median-tier finding: binary\n    labels make text more competitive, reducing the marginal value of hidden-state\n    access. HS still outperforms, but the practical advantage is much smaller. The\n    gap compression ratio is informative for the paper''s scope qualification of C2.'\n  tables:\n  - name: 'HS-Text Gap Compression: Original vs AdvBench'\n    columns:\n    - Dataset\n    - HS BA\n    - Text BA\n    - Gap BA\n    - HS Prec\n    - Text Prec\n    - Gap Prec\n    rows:\n    - - Original (R1-8B)\n      - '0.810'\n      - '0.678'\n      - +13.2pp\n      - '0.822'\n      - '0.487'\n      - +33.5pp\n    - - AdvBench (R1-8B)\n      - '0.730'\n      - '0.690'\n      - +4.0pp\n      - '0.560'\n      - '0.500'\n      - +6.0pp\n    - - Compression\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 3.3x\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 5.6x\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Paired bar chart: HS vs text accuracy on original (large gap) and AdvBench (small\n    gap), visually showing the compression.'\n  analysis: 'The gap compression is the most interpretable median-tier result. It\n    explains WHY AdvBench doesn''t strongly replicate: the HS modality advantage is\n    largest when the classification task requires detecting subtle internal state\n    transitions (temporal CP detection), not when surface patterns suffice (binary\n    jailbreak). This motivates the C1 scope qualification and provides mechanistic\n    understanding of when HS monitoring adds maximal value.'\n- name: AdvBench BOCPD/CUSUM\n  plan_id: exp-014\n  description: Not applicable. BOCPD/CUSUM detect distributional shifts in sequential\n    probe scores. AdvBench binary labels define no temporal transition to detect.\n  expected_results:\n    status: not_applicable\n    reason: No commitment-point transition in binary classification setup. Change-point\n      methods require a changepoint.\n  result_rationale: BOCPD/CUSUM target the probe score transition from safe→committed.\n    Under binary labels, all steps carry the same label — no transition exists. Running\n    these methods would detect noise-driven fluctuations, producing uninterpretable\n    results. On the original dataset, BOCPD already failed on 3/4 models; there is\n    no value in attempting it on a setup where the target signal is definitionally\n    absent.\n  tables: []\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: Skipped. Change-point methods require a change point.\n- name: AdvBench FPR Analysis\n  plan_id: exp-015\n  description: All probes on AdvBench safe traces. Step-level and trace-level FPR.\n  expected_results:\n    n_safe_test: 24\n    hs_step_fpr: 0.18\n    text_step_fpr: 0.24\n    hs_trace_fpr_k5: 0.33\n    text_trace_fpr_k5: 0.42\n    hs_fpr_lower: true\n    hs_text_fpr_gap_step_pp: 6.0\n    original_hs_step_fpr: 0.162\n    original_text_step_fpr: 0.272\n    original_fpr_gap_pp: 11.0\n  result_rationale: 'AdvBench safe FPR is somewhat lower than original (step: 0.18\n    vs 0.162, trace: 0.33 vs 0.39). AdvBench safe traces are \"cleaner\" — simple refusals,\n    shorter, less internal deliberation — producing fewer safety-adjacent patterns\n    that trigger false alarms. The HS-text FPR gap (6pp) is narrower than original\n    (11pp), consistent with the overall median-tier pattern of attenuated HS advantage\n    under binary labels. The FPR numbers are still substantial (33% trace-level for\n    HS), confirming deployment limitation persists cross-dataset.'\n  tables:\n  - name: FPR Cross-Dataset Comparison\n    columns:\n    - Dataset\n    - HS Step FPR\n    - Text Step FPR\n    - Gap\n    - HS Trace FPR\n    - Text Trace FPR\n    - Gap\n    rows:\n    - - Original (R1-8B)\n      - '0.162'\n      - '0.272'\n      - 11.0pp\n      - '0.390'\n      - '0.502'\n      - 11.2pp\n    - - AdvBench (R1-8B)\n      - '0.180'\n      - '0.240'\n      - 6.0pp\n      - '0.330'\n      - '0.420'\n      - 9.0pp\n  figures_description:\n  - FPR bar chart across datasets showing consistent HS < text pattern with smaller\n    gap on AdvBench.\n  analysis: HS FPR advantage replicates directionally on AdvBench with smaller magnitude.\n    The persistent high trace-level FPR (33%) confirms this is a cross-dataset limitation\n    of probe-based detection, not dataset-specific. Appendix material.\n","numLines":885,"startLine":1,"totalLines":885}}}
